gcm: GET_REPORT time stamps may leave out the walker's drain time (PS3RECOMP_GCM_REPORT_BUSY_SCALE).

Resistance 3 times its GPU frame with reports: besides its ~2360 occlusion queries a frame it puts marker ZPASS
reports at the start of the frame (IO 0x20B60), ~3 ms in (0x20B50) and at the end (0x20B40). func_0075A38C, called
from the frame function after it waits for the RSX, copies their time stamps and keeps last - first (ns) at
0x12B5238+0xE1C; func_003DCA40 turns it into ms and compares it with the frame budget times 1.33, 1.01, 0.90, 0.84 and
0.75 (a table at 0x3DCA20), setting and clearing the quality bits at 0x11DDFB4. Above the budget it drops decor: the
cloth hanging under the roofs and the bushes and saplings along the fences of the Haven fight came and went, and
stayed away whenever the RSX thread was slow. The walker stamped each report with the host's time when it reached
it, so the GPU time the game read was the host converting the frame's draws: 31-46 ms in the fight, quality bits
0x5; ~20 ms in calmer spans, bits 0x4.

The stamps now come from a clock (gcm_report_clock.h): the host's monotonic time less (1 - scale) of the time the
walker has spent in drain passes, rising strictly as before. PS3RECOMP_GCM_REPORT_BUSY_SCALE (0..1, read once) sets
the scale; 1, the default, is the host time as before; 0 is an RSX whose own work costs nothing, whose clock moves
only while it waits for commands, as a starved RSX's does. Time spent idle between passes is counted either way, so
a title that waits on GPU time while the walker has little to do sees it pass as before. The stamps then fall behind
the host's time by the drain time so far: a title that compares report stamps with CPU time should keep scale 1.

Found with a debug host (scripted boot to the fight): a run whose RSX thread was slowed (every vertex-cache hit
converted again for a check) showed no cloth and no bushes at all; the same run with the stamps compressed 20x from
t=150 s brought both back in the next snapshot. Compressing them from boot instead stalled the game at Press START,
which is why idle time keeps its full weight here. lldb read watchpoints found the readers above; nothing on the PPU
or SPU reads the occlusion queries' values or stamps.

The knob's text goes through gcm_report_clock_parse_scale: unset, empty or not wholly a number ("off", "0.5ms")
is 1, so a value meant to turn it off never turns it fully on (atof read "off" as 0).

Test: libs/video/tests/test_gcm_report_clock.c (scale 1 is the host time; scale 0 leaves a drain between two
reports out and counts the idle time before the next pass; 0.5 counts half; stamps rise strictly; 10000 random
passes, from the test's own xorshift so every platform runs the same ones, never go back or ahead of the host time
by more than the 1 ns a stamp needs to rise; a scale outside 0..1 or NaN is 1; the parser's cases). With the clock
reduced to the host time the test fails 2 checks, with the parser reduced to atof 4. Added to CMake
(gcm_report_clock) and to the macOS/Linux workflow steps.

Upstream: PR #753.
